# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two
# FILES, never a shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is
# defined in run_gates.sh. Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_extractor_determinism.py" "EXTRACTOR DETERMINISM GATE: PASS" "extractor determinism (graph bytes stable across PYTHONHASHSEED)" "extractor determinism: the serialized code graph is BYTE-IDENTICAL across runs over the IDENTICAL source tree. code_graph_extract.build_graph accumulates nodes/edges from many per-file/substrate passes, several of which collected table/symbol NAMES into a bare Python set and iterated that set DIRECTLY into node/edge emission (_cg_schema_orm._orm_table_refs feeding _cg_schema._schema_graph's for-t-in-orm_tables edge loop, the _cg_schema_go._go_* struct/table fns, the SQL/creator ref fns, plus the routes/config/iac/openapi/api-contract passes). A bare set's STRING iteration order is randomized by PYTHONHASHSEED, so two runs over the same tree produced the SAME node/edge SET but a DIFFERENT serialized ORDER -- DIFFERENT BYTES -- making every byte-sensitive consumer (graph diffs, what-changed surfaces, freshness fingerprints, change detection) churn on every push for NO real change (a whole invisible regression class + a latent reproducibility flake). FIXED two ways, both CONTENT-FREE (order only; the node/edge SET is unchanged): (1) every ref-producing extractor fn that returns/iterates a set now emits in a STABLE order (return sorted(out)) in _orm_table_refs / _orm_creator_refs / _rails_column_ddl_refs / _go_explicit_refs / _go_gorm_struct_names / _go_ent_schema_names / _sql_creator_refs; and (2) belt-and-suspenders -- build_graph performs a final canonical sort of the assembled nodes (by id) and edges (by (src,dst,kind)) BEFORE return, so the OUTPUT is canonical regardless of any upstream insertion order, closing the regression class structurally. This gate writes ONE rich multi-substrate fixture (many ORM models in one file, many gorm structs in one Go file, raw .sql CREATE TABLEs, raw-SQL queriers -- enough distinct NAMES that an unsorted set's order genuinely differs between seeds), runs the FULL extractor TWICE in CHILD processes pinned to DIFFERENT PYTHONHASHSEED (0 then 1), and asserts the serialized graph JSON is BYTE-IDENTICAL. It ALSO asserts the node/edge SET is identical across seeds (proving the fix is a PURE reorder, never a content change) and that the emitted output is in canonical (sorted) order. PROVEN to FAIL pre-fix (the two seeds reorder the ORM/Go refs -- first divergence table::m4 vs table::m9 -- so the bytes differ) and PASS post-fix. Content-free throughout (table/struct NAMES + file paths only, never bodies or values)" "extractor non-determinism is closed: the serialized code graph is now byte-identical across PYTHONHASHSEED so graph diffs / what-changed / freshness fingerprints no longer churn on every push for no real change -- ref-producing set fns emit sorted + build_graph canonicalizes node/edge order before return, a pure content-free reorder verified to FAIL pre-fix and PASS post-fix across two hash seeds" 24
